      "chapterTitle": "I. The Blackout Sirens and the Crowded Table (1944–1949)",
      "heading": "I. The Blackout Sirens and the Crowded Table (1944–1949)",
      "body": "The house at 14 Balaram Ghosh Street was never designed for silence.\n\nIn November of 1944, when Nirmalendu Roy drew his first breath in the ground-floor bedroom adjoining the southern courtyard, twenty-two members of the Roy clan lived beneath its sixteen-foot timber ceilings. The Second World War was rumbling across the eastern frontier in Burma, and Japanese reconnaissance aircraft—twin-engine Mitsubishi Ki-46s flying high and thin above the Bay of Bengal—occasionally glinted like slivers of tin in the hazy winter skies above the Hooghly. Every evening at five o'clock, his father, Dhirendranath Roy, walked through the three-story mansion with a long bamboo pole tipped with a brass hook, pulling down heavy black oilcloth blinds across sixty-four tall louvered windows to comply with the British Air Raid Precaution regulations.\n\nTo an infant growing into a toddler, the world did not smell of cordite or geopolitics; it smelled of cooling lime plaster, boiled mustard oil, tobacco from his grandfather’s silver-mounted narghile hookah, and the sweet, resinous warmth of seasoned Burma teak rafters that groaned gently whenever the northern winter wind swept down from the river plains of Nadia.\n\nThe mansion had been built in 1892 by Nirmal's grandfather, Radhakanta Roy, who had made a respectable fortune as a banian supplier of sal timber and river barges to the East India Railway company. Radhakanta had built in the classical North Kolkata hybrid style—thick brick masonry laid in surki mortar made from crushed burnt clay, semicircular Roman arches resting on fluted cast-iron pillars imported from Glasgow, and deep green-painted wooden Venetian louvers that could tilt to admit the monsoon draft while keeping out the blinding glare of the Bengal summer.\n\nThe absolute heart of the mansion was the inner quadrangle. Paved with square Chunar flagstones that retained the chill of the earth well into April, it opened straight to the sky like a stone throat. Around this light-well, two tiers of wrought-iron verandahs wrapped the family life in a continuous domestic theatre.\n\nOn the ground floor, three widowed great-aunts—elderly women in coarse unbordered white handloom who had lost husbands in the cholera epidemics of the 1910s—spent their mornings sitting on woven palm mats. They operated heavy iron boti slicers held down by the weight of their right thighs, cutting mounds of winter cauliflower, green plantains, and pumpkin into geometries of absolute culinary precision. Behind them, rows of earthen jars containing mango pickles and dried lentil dumplings (*bori*) sunned themselves in the patches of light that crawled slowly across the stone floor.\n\nOn the first floor, Nirmal’s mother, Saralabala, oversaw the vast copper handis of rice and split-pea dal that boiled continuously on twin coal ovens built into the masonry wall. To feed twenty-two people twice a day required fifty pounds of parboiled rice every week, forty pints of fresh cow's milk delivered in tin churns from the Baghbazar stables, and an unbroken discipline of domestic management that resembled the logistical command of a military battalion.\n\nIn August of 1947, when Nirmal was nearly three, the sirens stopped for good.\n\nHe remembered being held high on his father’s shoulders at the intersection of Bidhan Sarani and Shyambazar five-point crossing. A sea of khadi caps, torchlights, and roaring voices washed around them beneath the bronze equestrian statue of Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose. People were shouting *Jai Hind* until their throats cracked, weep-laughing in the warm monsoon rain, embracing strangers, and handing out clay cups of boiling sweet tea from roadside stalls.\n\nYet what remained etched into the boy’s memory was not the grand political dawn of a newly independent nation; it was the return to the house that midnight.\n\nHis father had walked into the grand entrance hallway, removed the dark ARP blackout curtains from the high fanlights with his own bare hands, and hurled the stiff oilcloth into the courtyard. For the first time in Nirmal’s conscious life, the full yellow moonlight poured through the leaded glass arch, washing the black-and-white Italian marble floor in long ribbons of silver.\n\n\"Look, Nirmal,\" Dhirendranath had said, his deep voice trembling as he held the child's hand. \"The house can see the sky again. Never let anyone cover its eyes.\"",
