      "id": "sec-hj-1b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "I-B. The Parijat Sapling and the German Clock (1950–1953)",
      "heading": "I-B. The Parijat Sapling and the German Clock (1950–1953)",
      "body": "In the monsoon of 1950, when Nirmal was five, Saralabala took him by the hand one Saturday morning and walked down the cobbled lane to Baghbazar Ghat.\n\nThe river was swollen with muddy rain from the Chota Nagpur hills, carrying clumps of green water hyacinth past the bathing steps. At the riverside plant nursery operated by an old gardener named Paran, Saralabala selected an earthen pot no larger than a teacup. It contained a frail, six-inch twig with five pale green leaves: a parijat sapling (*Nyctanthes arbor-tristis*).\n\n\"Hold it with both hands, Nirmal,\" she instructed as they walked back through the narrow lanes of Shyambazar, past the sweetshops smelling of boiling sugar syrup and fried chhena. \"The roots of a parijat are as delicate as a newborn's veins. If you shake them, the plant dies of shock.\"\n\nIn the inner quadrangle, in the southeastern corner where the morning sun lingered longest before the shadow of the three-story wall fell across the flags, father and son dug a pit two feet deep. They removed the hard brick rubble and replaced it with rich river silt mixed with well-rotted cowdung and leaf mould.\n\nWhen the sapling was placed upright into the pit, Saralabala brought a small brass pitcher filled with water from the Ganga. She placed Nirmal’s small hands around the neck of the vessel, guiding his fingers as the cool water poured over the dark earth.\n\n\"This tree will live long after both of us are gone, Nirmal,\" his mother told him, kneeling beside him on the wet flagstones, wiping the dark mud from his wrists with the hem of her sari. \"In thirty years, when autumn arrives, you will step out into this courtyard at dawn and the stones will be covered in white blossoms with bright orange stems. Remember this rule: you must never shake the branches to make them fall. The parijat is a tree of sorrow and beauty. It only gives its perfume to flowers that drop of their own free will.\"\n\nThat same week, a heavy wooden crate arrived by bullock cart from the docks at Strand Road.\n\nInside, packed in excelsior straw and greased paper, was an eight-day German regulator wall clock manufactured by Junghans in the Black Forest in 1928. Radhakanta had ordered it before his death, but the disruptions of the war had delayed its delivery through the port customs for six years.\n\nIt was a magnificent instrument. The octagonal case was carved from dark European walnut with arched pediments and fluted side columns. The ivory dial was twelve inches across, marked with bold Roman numerals and delicate pierced steel hands shaped like spade leaves. Behind the glass door hung a heavy, circular brass pendulum bob with a turned adjusting nut, suspended by a thin spring steel feather.\n\nDhirendranath took a masonry drill and two copper spikes and mounted the clock upon the load-bearing brick wall of the central family salon on the first floor.\n\nHe called Nirmal to his side. In his palm lay the heavy brass winding key, forged with two symmetrical wings shaped like the flukes of a ship's anchor.\n\n\"Look closely, Nirmal,\" his father said, inserting the square socket of the key into the right-hand winding arbor.\n\n*Crrk... crrk... crrk... crrk.*\n\nThe ratchet pawl clicked with a crisp, oily precision that echoed against the high plaster ceiling.\n\n\"Every Sunday morning at eight o'clock,\" his father declared, looking into the boy's wide brown eyes. \"You wind the right arbor until the spring resists—that powers the escapement. Then you wind the left arbor—that powers the half-hour strike. If you neglect the winding, the pendulum stops. If the pendulum stops, the house loses its heartbeat. Remember that, Nirmal. A house that loses its rhythm is already halfway to the graveyard.\"\n\nFrom that Sunday forward, the cadence of Nirmal's childhood was set. Every seven days, the brass key was turned. Every half hour, two coiled steel gongs vibrated with a deep, cathedral-like chime: *bong... bong...* that could be heard through every bedroom, every corridor, and down into the courtyard flagstones where the parijat sapling was putting down its first deep roots into the Kolkata earth."
