      "id": "sec-hj-3",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II. The First Notch and the Southern Roof (1954–1960)",
      "heading": "II. The First Notch and the Southern Roof (1954–1960)",
      "body": "By the winter of 1954, Nirmal was ten years old. He was skinny, knobby-kneed, with dark brown eyes that missed nothing and thick unruly hair that defied the fragrant coconut oil his mother pressed into his scalp every morning before school.\n\nOn his tenth birthday, the fourteenth of November, his father summoned him into the ground-floor library.\n\nThe library was a long, narrow room with high timber shelves holding leather-bound volumes of the *Encyclopaedia Britannica*, the district gazetteers of Bengal, and the cloth-bound ledgers of the family’s modest landholdings in Nadia. The air smelled of old paper, camphor pellets, and the sweet, dry scent of seasoned teak.\n\n\"Take off your slippers, Nirmal,\" Dhirendranath said, standing beside the left jamb of the heavy inner doorway. \"Stand with your heels flat against the skirting board. Shoulders back. Chin level. Look directly at the portrait of your grandfather over the desk.\"\n\nNirmal stood stiffly against the doorframe. The old Burma teak was cold against the cotton of his kurta.\n\nHis father took from his waistcoat pocket a small folding pocketknife with handles carved from yellowed cow bone. He opened the sharp spear-point carbon steel blade. With a steady, deliberate motion, Dhirendranath pressed the flat of the blade against the top of Nirmal's head and made a clean, horizontal incision three-eighths of an inch deep into the timber.\n\nHe folded the knife, dipped an ebony-handled dip pen into a bottle of purple iron-gall ink, and wrote in small, elegant copperplate script directly beneath the freshly carved groove:\n\n*14.11.1954 — Nirmal — 4 ft 3 in.*\n\n\"Every year on your birthday, until you stop growing, we measure,\" his father said, blowing lightly on the wet purple ink to dry it. \"The doorframe will keep the record. Clothes wear out, photographs fade in the damp, but seasoned teak remembers the exact height of a man.\"\n\nTwo years later, in the scorching May of 1956, Calcutta was struck by an unrelenting heatwave.\n\nThe mercury touched 114 degrees Fahrenheit in the shade. The thick brick walls of Shyambazar absorbed the solar radiation all day and re-radiated it throughout the night like baking ovens. The punkah-wallah hired to pull the canvas fans on the second floor fell asleep from heat exhaustion at midnight. The bedrooms became stifling, oxygen-starved chambers where sleep was impossible.\n\nAt two in the morning, suffocating in his bed, twelve-year-old Nirmal crept out into the dark corridor.\n\nHe climbed the spiral cast-iron staircase at the end of the western verandah, ascending through the three levels until he reached the heavy wrought-iron gate that barred the doorway to the flat southern terrace roof. The gate had been padlocked for three years, ever since his grandfather had grown too frail to climb the stairs.\n\nNirmal knew where the key hung. Behind the zinc water cistern on the upper landing, hidden behind a loose masonry brick, was an ancient iron nail. On that nail hung a massive, four-inch brass key, its bow forged in the shape of a trefoil, its heavy rectangular bit cut with four intricate steps.\n\nHe took the key down. It was heavy in his hand, smelling of oxidized copper and dust.\n\nHe slid the bit into the keyhole of the English Chubb rim-lock mounted on the iron frame. He grasped the bow with both hands, braced his bare feet against the stone threshold, and turned with all his weight.\n\nFor a second, the mechanism resisted. Then, with a deep, hydraulic *clack* that sounded like a rifle bolt sliding home, the four brass tumblers lifted. The iron gate swung open into the night.\n\nNirmal stepped onto the open roof.\n\nThe change was instant and staggering. The southern wind, blowing fresh and cool from the Bay of Bengal seventy miles to the south, washed across the terrace parapet like a breaker of cold seawater. It smelled of river mud, mangrove vegetation, salt flats, and wet rope.\n\nAbove him, the night sky was not the pale, washed-out grey of modern electric cities, but an immense, velvety dome of deepest indigo, strewn with millions of cold, glittering stars. Below, Calcutta lay sleeping under the warm yellow pinpricks of gas lanterns along Bidhan Sarani and Grey Street. To the west, two miles away, the dark Hooghly River curved through the night, reflecting the glowing red firebox of an engine shunting coal at the Chitpur rail yards.\n\nNirmal sat on the wide brick parapet until five in the morning, his bare legs dangling over the alleyway, watching the black chimney smoke of the Cossipore generating station drift across the constellations of Orion and the Pleiades.\n\nHe listened to the first temple bells ringing from the river ghats at Baghbazar, the distant cries of fishermen hauling their nets, the early morning trundle of the first empty tram car rattling over the points at the five-point crossing.\n\nBefore he went downstairs, he took a three-cornered file he had stolen from his uncle's carpentry box and made a deep, sharp V-shaped notch across the brass shaft of the terrace key.\n\nIt was his secret signature. The key was no longer just his grandfather's property; it was his private passport to the wind, the sky, and the river.",
