      "id": "sec-hj-3c",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-C. The James and Mary Shoal and the Octant of Moulmein Teak (1962–1964)",
      "heading": "II-C. The James and Mary Shoal and the Octant of Moulmein Teak (1962–1964)",
      "body": "Between 1962 and 1964, Nirmal's apprenticeship aboard the Port Trust survey vessel *Pathfinder* brought him face-to-face with the most treacherous hydrographic battleground in the subcontinent: the dreaded James and Mary Shoal.\n\nLocated thirty miles south of Calcutta, at the fateful confluence where the Hooghly meets the mighty tidal waters of the Rupnarayan and Damodar rivers, the James and Mary was a graveyard of merchant shipping. The complex hydrodynamics of the three colliding currents created massive underwater vortexes that sucked millions of tons of fine silt into shifting underwater dunes. A channel that had thirty feet of water at morning high tide could silt up to fourteen feet by mid-afternoon, creating an underwater shelf of quicksand so voracious that any vessel touching it would be rolled over by the eight-knot current and buried beneath the silt within forty-eight hours.\n\nIt was named after the Royal James and Mary, an English merchantman of five hundred tons that had run aground on the shoal in September of 1694 and disappeared beneath the waves with all hands in less than half an hour.\n\n\"Roy!\" Captain Eric Macfarlane shouted from the open bridge wing of the *Pathfinder* on a stormy September morning in 1963. \"Look at that tide gauge at Hooghly Point! The bore is coming in thirty minutes! We need sounding line number four completed before the wave hits, or the Clan Line steamer waiting at Saugor will miss her draft window!\"\n\nNirmal stood on the starboard sounding platform, soaked to the bone in spray and driving monsoon rain.\n\nHe held the fourteen-pound lead sounding weight by its braided hemp line. The line was marked every fathom with strips of leather, white bunting, and red bunting. With a rhythmic, powerful swing of his right arm, Nirmal whirled the lead weight in two complete vertical circles and hurled it sixty feet forward into the churning brown foam.\n\n*Whuff.* The lead struck the water.\n\nAs the launch surged forward, the line came vertical beneath his hands. Nirmal felt the lead strike the hard river sand twelve fathoms below. His fingers read the vibrations through the wet hemp with the sensitivity of a violinist feeling the resonance of a string.\n\n\"Mark five!\" Nirmal bellowed toward the chartroom, his voice cutting through the scream of the gale. \"Twenty-nine feet six inches! Hard sand with black mud!\"\n\nIn the chartroom, balancing against the violent roll of the vessel, Nirmal plotted the sounding coordinates onto a massive sheet of handmade Whatman paper using an antique brass octant with an ebony frame that had belonged to his father.\n\nThe octant was a masterwork of nineteenth-century instrument-making, its silver vernier scale graduated to ten seconds of arc, its mirrors silvered with mercury. With this instrument, taking rapid double-angles between shore beacons at Nurpur and Gewankhali, Nirmal fixed the position of the sounding launch within three yards of absolute accuracy.\n\nThe hydrographic surveys of the Hooghly were an unforgiving school of character.\n\nOn the river, there was no room for rhetoric, no room for political compromise, no room for bureaucratic hesitation. A depth was either twenty-eight feet or it was twenty-two feet. If a surveyor made an error of twelve inches in his sounding sheet, a cargo ship carrying five thousand tons of export tea from Darjeeling would ground, her hull would buckle, and the entire port of Calcutta would be paralyzed for weeks.\n\nNirmal learned to trust only what could be measured, verified, and recorded with absolute fidelity.\n\nYet he also learned that nature was never a static adversary to be conquered, but an immense living system that demanded humility, vigilance, and profound respect. The river followed the pull of the moon; it carved its channels according to seasonal laws that predated human civilization by millions of years.\n\nWhen Nirmal returned to Shyambazar on Saturday evenings after five days on the survey launch, he brought that same quiet, uncompromising exactitude home to 14 Balaram Ghosh Street.\n\nHe inspected the masonry plinths for damp with the same analytical eye he used to inspect sandbanks; he oiled the winding gears of the Junghans clock with the same delicate precision he used to lubricate the micrometer screws of his sextant. The house and the river were two halves of the same discipline: both were physical structures subject to the relentless forces of time, water, and gravity, requiring human vigilance and devotion to remain upright in the storm."
