      "chapterTitle": "III. The Red Footprints of Chinsurah (1968–1973)",
      "heading": "III. The Red Footprints of Chinsurah (1968–1973)",
      "body": "In December of 1968, the sound of the shehnai returned to 14 Balaram Ghosh Street.\n\nHer name was Kalyani. She was nineteen years old, the daughter of a scholarly schoolmaster from Chinsurah who taught classical Sanskrit at the Hooghly Mohsin College. She had large, calm hazel eyes, thick black hair that fell to her waist in a heavy braid, small capable hands that knew how to play the tanpura, and a quiet, dignified composure that seemed to smooth the rough edges of any room she entered.\n\nOn the evening of their wedding reception, Nirmal watched her arrive in the hired black Ambassador car decorated with white tuberose garlands.\n\nShe was dressed in a crimson Benarasi silk sari woven with real gold zari in the traditional paisley pattern (*kalka*). Her forehead was painted with delicate dots of white sandalwood paste curving above her eyebrows; a large gold nath ring hung from her left nostril, catching the light of the incandescent bulbs strung along the alleyway.\n\nIn the entrance hall, as tradition demanded, she was welcomed by Saralabala.\n\nAt the marble threshold, Kalyani stepped her right foot into a flat bronze thali filled with scarlet *alta* dye mixed with milk. Then, stepping forward onto a long runner of white handloom linen that had been spread across the black-and-white marble floor, she walked toward the inner courtyard.\n\nHer tiny red footprints stretched in an unbroken line from the street doorway to the quadrangle.\n\nWhere the linen runner ended, she paused.\n\nBefore her stood the parijat tree. It was now eighteen years old, its trunk thick and gnarled, its umbrella of dark green foliage rising two stories high into the night sky. The season of blooming was almost over, but a dozen late white flowers with vivid orange calyxes had dropped onto the grey Chunar flagstones during the evening.\n\nWithout being prompted by the priests or the elderly women, Kalyani knelt down on the cold stones, heedless of her heavy wedding silk. She picked up three fallen blossoms with her fingertips, touched them reverently to her forehead, and tucked them into the gold-bordered fold of her sari at her waist.\n\nUp on the first-floor verandah, Saralabala wiped her eyes with the corner of her widow's sari.\n\n\"She understands the tree,\" the old mother murmured to her neighbor. \"The house will not be lonely anymore.\"\n\nKalyani brought a profound, orderly warmth into the cavernous rooms.\n\nWithin a month, she had learned the peculiarities of the mansion as if she had lived there since birth. She knew which window latch stuck when the humidity rose above eighty percent; she knew that the third step on the marble staircase was slightly chipped and needed to be navigated in the dark; she knew that the kitchen chimney drew best when the south wind was blowing from the river.\n\nIn September of 1970, in the same ground-floor bedroom where Nirmal had been born twenty-six years earlier, their daughter was born.\n\nThey named her Soma.\n\nOn the morning she turned one year old, Nirmal brought his wife and infant daughter into the library.\n\nHe stood beside the Burma teak doorframe. Above his own childhood notches from the 1950s—where the highest mark read *14.11.1962 — Nirmal — 5 ft 9 in*—he placed the baby's heel gently against the baseboard while Kalyani held her upright.\n\nWith the bone-handled pocketknife, Nirmal made a fresh, tiny notch three inches above the lower molding. With the purple ink, he wrote:\n\n*14.09.1971 — Soma — 2 ft 4 in.*\n\nKalyani leaned her head against Nirmal's arm, watching the ink sink into the yellow pores of the teak.\n\n\"Do you think she will be tall, Nirmal?\" Kalyani asked.\n\n\"She will be as tall as her spirit wants to be,\" Nirmal smiled, kissing his wife's temple. \"And the door will record every inch of it.\"",
