      "id": "sec-hj-7c",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "IV-C. The Alpona of Winter and the Saraswati Puja (1987–1990)",
      "heading": "IV-C. The Alpona of Winter and the Saraswati Puja (1987–1990)",
      "body": "In the winter of 1989, when Soma was nineteen and Aniruddha fifteen, the annual festival of Saraswati Puja transformed 14 Balaram Ghosh Street into a temple of light and art.\n\nFor three generations, the Roy family had celebrated the worship of the goddess of learning in the inner quadrangle. Unlike the noisy public street pujas with their blaring cinema loudspeakers and garish plaster idols, the puja at Balaram Ghosh Street was an intimate, monastic festival of intellect, literature, and domestic beauty.\n\nThe preparations began three days before the auspicious fifth day of the waxing moon (*Vasant Panchami*).\n\nOn the morning before the festival, Kalyani took two pounds of fragrant Gobindobhog rice, soaked it in Ganga water for four hours, and ground it into a silky, milk-white paste (*pituli*) on the heavy black granite grinding stone (*shil-nora*) in the courtyard pantry.\n\nAt sunset, Kalyani knelt upon the cold Chunar flagstones of the quadrangle.\n\nShe dipped a small wad of washed cotton cloth into the white rice paste, holding it delicately between her thumb and middle finger. Moving backward across the courtyard with practiced, fluid grace, she began to paint the *alpona*—the traditional ritual floor painting that was the ancestral heritage of Bengal's women.\n\nNirmal stood on the first-floor verandah, looking down at his wife.\n\nBeneath her fingers, the grey stones blossomed into an astonishing, intricate tapestry of white geometric art. She painted creeping vines of parijat flowers with five-pointed leaves, pairs of sacred conch shells (*shankha*), the footprints of the goddess surrounded by circular lotuses, and the elegant, stylized forms of white swans gliding across rippling water.\n\nThe lines were astonishingly sharp, flowing from her fingertips with the effortless precision of a master calligrapher.\n\n\"Come down, Soma,\" Kalyani called up softly, her forehead glistening with fine beads of sweat. \"It is time you took over the outer border.\"\n\nSoma, then an undergraduate at Presidency College, knelt beside her mother on the stones. For three hours, mother and daughter worked side by side in the lantern light, their white handloom saris tucked tightly at the waist, their fingers tracing the sacred patterns across fifty square yards of stone.\n\nBy midnight, the inner quadrangle was completely covered in a shimmering, lace-like carpet of white rice art that glowed like frost under the winter moon.\n\nAt dawn on the day of the puja, the clay idol of Saraswati arrived from the potters' quarter of Kumartuli, carried on a wooden bier by four young craftsmen.\n\nThe goddess was seated upon a white swan, holding a polished veena in her hands, her face sculpted with calm, benevolent serenity from the sacred alluvial clay of the Hooghly. She was installed on a raised wooden dais beneath the parijat tree, whose branches were adorned with garlands of yellow marigolds and green mango leaves.\n\nThen came the ritual that defined the intellectual soul of the household: the *Boi-Pujo*—the blessing of the books.\n\nOn the steps of the dais, beneath the feet of the goddess, the family stacked the tools of their minds:\n\nNirmal placed his brass surveyor's octant, his tidal prediction tables, and his grandfather's 1892 Moulmein timber ledger.\n\nKalyani placed her mother's palm-leaf manuscript of the *Bhagavad Gita* and her silver-inlaid tanpura.\n\nSoma placed her university textbooks on macroeconomics, her volumes of Amartya Sen, and her well-thumbed copies of John Maynard Keynes.\n\nYoung Aniruddha placed his sliderule, his German geometry compass set, his calculus notebooks, and a small copper electric motor he had wound with his own hands.\n\nBeside the instruments of learning, two dozen glass inkwells filled with black, blue, and purple ink were placed upon the rice alpona, each holding an unsharpened quill or a bamboo dip pen (*khager kolom*).\n\nWhen the family priest, an elderly Brahmin from Baghbazar with a voice like a bronze bell, chanted the ancient Vedic mantras, the courtyard echoed with the sacred Sanskrit verses:\n\n*Saraswati namastubhyam, varade kamarupini...*\n\n\"O Saraswati, salute to thee, giver of boons, who fulfills all desires. I begin my studies, may there be accomplishment for me always.\"\n\nNirmal stood beside his children, his palms joined in prayer.\n\nHe looked at the books stacked beneath the clay feet of the goddess—the ledgers of his grandfather, the instruments of his own river career, the economic treatises of his daughter, the mathematical calculations of his son.\n\nFour generations of one family had pursued knowledge not for ostentation or brute accumulation, but as a sacred dialogue with truth.\n\nAnd around them, the lime-plastered walls and seasoned teak rafters of 14 Balaram Ghosh Street held the chanting, held the fragrance of burning sandalwood and roasted yellow moong dal, holding the family in an embrace that felt as enduring as the river that flowed three blocks to the west."
