      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "V. The Retirement and the Golden Autumn (2000–2010)",
      "heading": "V. The Retirement and the Golden Autumn (2000–2010)",
      "body": "In December of 2004, at the age of sixty, Nirmalendu Roy retired from the Calcutta Port Trust after forty-three years of continuous service.\n\nHis retirement ceremony was held at the Marine Club on Strand Road. In the presence of eighty river pilots, hydrographers, and port engineers, the Chairman presented Nirmal with a silver-plated scale model of the survey vessel *Pathfinder* and a leather-bound citation commending his exceptional forty-three-year contribution to the safety of navigation on the Hooghly River.\n\nAt four o'clock that afternoon, Nirmal boarded the tram for the last time as an active government servant.\n\nHe carried the silver ship in a cardboard box under his arm. He stepped down at Shyambazar, walked down the familiar cobblestones of Balaram Ghosh Street, and pushed open the heavy mahogany front door.\n\nThe house was breathtakingly quiet.\n\nIn 1944, twenty-two voices had echoed across the courtyard. In 1978, children had shouted and raced down the corridors. Now, there was only the steady, wooden ticking of the Junghans clock on the first-floor landing, and the faint, comforting clatter of a brass spoon against an iron saucepan in the kitchen.\n\nKalyani came out to the hallway, wiping her hands on her apron.\n\nShe looked at the cardboard box in his hands, then looked into his eyes. A gentle, knowing smile touched her lips.\n\n\"The sailor has come home,\" she said softly.\n\n\"Yes,\" Nirmal smiled, setting the silver ship down on the marble console table. \"The voyage is finished.\"\n\nThe decade that followed was the quiet, golden autumn of their lives.\n\nThey settled into an unhurried, monastic routine of two people who had loved each other so deeply and for so long that words had become largely superfluous.\n\nEvery morning at five-thirty, Nirmal walked down to Baghbazar Ghat. He took his swim in the cool, flowing water of the Hooghly, watching the morning mists rise from the river as the country boats brought fresh vegetables from the rural districts of Nadia and Hooghly. He returned home carrying a bunch of fresh coriander, sweet winter radishes, and two live pond-carp wrapped in fresh banana leaves.\n\nKalyani brewed tea in two white porcelain cups—strong CTC tea brewed with crushed ginger, whole green cardamom, and fresh cow's milk.\n\nThey drank their tea together on the courtyard verandah, sitting side by side on two cane chairs, watching the white parijat blossoms drift down from the canopy onto the Chunar flagstones.\n\nIn the afternoons, Nirmal took over the physical maintenance of the house with the same methodical precision he had once applied to hydrographic charts. He scraped the lime efflorescence from the brick foundations; he oiled the bronze hinges of the sixty-four louvered shutters with linseed oil; he weeded the terrace parapets; every Sunday morning at eight o'clock, with absolute religious fidelity, he stood on his footstool and wound the Junghans clock.\n\nThe children visited every winter.\n\nSoma brought her husband and two teenage daughters from Bangalore. Aniruddha, now a senior research director at a robotics firm in America, flew in from Ohio with his wife and their five-year-old daughter, Rimli.\n\nFor three weeks every December, the old mansion shook off its silence and erupted into joyous chaos.\n\nRimli, speaking English with an American Midwestern cadence, was enchanted by the high ceilings, the echo of the stairwell, and the ancient bronze handles.\n\nOn a sunlit morning in December 2005, Nirmal took his granddaughter by the hand and led her to the library doorframe.\n\n\"Look here, Rimli,\" he smiled, pointing to the dense thicket of marks. \"Here is your father when he was five. And here is your Aunt Soma. Now it is your turn.\"\n\nHe placed the little girl's heels against the teak. With the bone-handled knife, he cut a tiny, perfect groove:\n\n*22.12.2005 — Rimli — 5 yrs — 3 ft 5 in.*\n\nRimli reached up and touched the groove with her small, smooth thumb.\n\n\"Grandpa,\" she asked, looking up with wide eyes. \"Will this mark stay here forever?\"\n\n\"As long as the house stands, little bird,\" Nirmal whispered. \"It will never leave.\"",
