      "id": "sec-hj-9b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "V-B. The Nor'wester of 2012 and the Final Clock-Winding (2011–2016)",
      "heading": "V-B. The Nor'wester of 2012 and the Final Clock-Winding (2011–2016)",
      "body": "Then came the bitter, devastating spring of 2011.\n\nIt began innocently, with a mild, persistent ache beneath Kalyani's right ribs and an unexplained fatigue that she tried to dismiss as ordinary indigestion from summer heat. By July, however, her appetite had vanished, and her skin had taken on a pale, translucent, lemon-yellow hue.\n\nNirmal took her to the medical college hospital on College Street.\n\nThe ultrasound and biopsy confirmed their worst fears: advanced cholangiocarcinoma—inoperable cancer of the bile ducts. The chief oncologist, an old friend from Scottish Church College, took Nirmal into his private office, closed the door, and placed a hand on his shoulder.\n\n\"Three to six months, Nirmal,\" the doctor said quietly. \"Keep her comfortable. Do not subject her to painful interventions that offer no cure.\"\n\nNirmal walked out of the hospital into the blinding July sun. He did not cry. He felt only an immense, cold clarity settle over his soul.\n\nHe refused to place Kalyani in a sterile hospice or hire professional nurses.\n\nAt sixty-seven, he transformed himself into her full-time caregiver. He moved their marriage bed down from the second floor to the ground-floor bedroom adjoining the courtyard, so that Kalyani could look out at her beloved parijat tree without having to climb the marble stairs.\n\nHe bathed her frail body with warm water scented with rose petals; he prepared light gruels of sago and green papaya with his own hands; he crushed her painkillers with a brass mortar and pestle; during the long, suffocating nights of the Bengal summer, when the electricity failed and the ceiling fan died, he sat beside her bed with a palm-leaf hand fan, fanning her rhythmically for hours until she fell into a fitful sleep.\n\nIn April of 2012, during the season of the violent nor'wester thunderstorms (*Kalbaishakhi*), the end arrived.\n\nOn the night of the sixteenth of April, a massive tempest broke over Kolkata. The wind roared at sixty miles an hour, driving torrents of rain against the green shutters, bending the thick branches of the parijat tree, rattling the heavy glass panes of the skylights like machine-gun fire. The city grid collapsed, and total darkness enveloped Shyambazar.\n\nIn the bedroom, two brass oil lamps cast flickering golden circles on the lime-plastered walls.\n\nKalyani was slipping away. Her breathing was shallow, rapid, and raspy; her pulse was a faint, thready flutter beneath Nirmal's fingertips.\n\nAround two in the morning, as the roar of the wind subsided into a steady, rhythmic drumbeat of rain upon the courtyard flagstones, Kalyani opened her eyes.\n\nHer gaze, which had been clouded by morphine, suddenly cleared. She looked at the shadows dancing across the ceiling, then turned her face toward Nirmal.\n\nShe reached out with her cold, emaciated right hand and touched Nirmal’s wrist.\n\n\"Nirmal,\" she whispered, her voice barely louder than the rustle of rain.\n\nNirmal leaned down, kissing her pale, dry lips. \"I am here, Kalyani. I am right here beside you.\"\n\n\"The clock upstairs...\" she breathed, her chest heaving with the effort. \"Did you... did you wind it this morning?\"\n\nNirmal felt a sudden, crushing spasm of agony in his heart.\n\nIn the terrifying panic of her sudden decline over the preceding forty-eight hours, he had forgotten the Sunday ritual. For the first time in sixty-two years, the Junghans clock had run down. Its pendulum was still; its springs were slack.\n\n\"I will wind it, Kalyani,\" Nirmal wept, his tears falling onto her cold hands. \"I will wind it this very second.\"\n\nA faint, exquisitely peaceful smile curved the corners of Kalyani’s lips.\n\n\"Good,\" she whispered, closing her eyes. \"A house must not stop... while we are in it... keep the heartbeat... going...\"\n\nHer hand grew slack in his palm. Her breathing softened, caught once, and gently ceased.\n\nKalyani was sixty-three years old.\n\nWhen the relatives and neighbors arrived at dawn with the bamboo bier, the white handloom shroud, and the garlands of tuberoses, they found the front door unlocked.\n\nThey walked up the marble staircase to the first floor.\n\nThere, in the central salon, stood Nirmalendu Roy.\n\nHe had bathed in cold well water. He wore a clean white cotton dhoti. His face was pale as sculpted marble, but his hands were steady. He was standing on the wooden footstool before the Junghans clock, holding the winged brass key.\n\nHe inserted the key into the right arbor.\n\n*Crrk... crrk... crrk... crrk.*\n\nHe wound the left strike barrel. He took the heavy brass pendulum bob between his fingers, pulled it to the side, and released it.\n\n*Tock... tick... tock... tick.*\n\nThe deep, solemn wooden chime filled the empty mansion.\n\nOnly then did Nirmal step down from the stool, walk down the stairs, and let the mourners carry his wife out to the river."
