      "chapterTitle": "V-C. The Monsoon Maintenance and the Terrace Gargoyles (2013–2016)",
      "heading": "V-C. The Monsoon Maintenance and the Terrace Gargoyles (2013–2016)",
      "body": "In the years immediately following Kalyani's death, the physical maintenance of the roof became Nirmal's primary summer liturgy.\n\nEvery year in late May, before the first monsoon depression formed over the Andaman Sea, Nirmal prepared 14 Balaram Ghosh Street for the deluge. The flat southern terrace roof was seventy feet by forty feet, covered in square terracotta tiles laid in a bed of surki mortar. Drainage was provided by six cast-iron gargoyles shaped like makara sea monsters, projecting eighteen inches beyond the brick cornice to throw the runoff clear of the exterior walls.\n\nAt seventy years of age, Nirmal climbed the spiral iron staircase carrying a wooden bucket of coal tar, a stiff coir brush, and a bamboo clearing rod.\n\nHe unlocked the terrace gate with his notched brass key.\n\nHe knelt on the terracotta tiles under the heavy, purple-grey pre-monsoon sky. With the bamboo rod, he cleared each makara mouth of dead parijat leaves, bird droppings, and accumulated dust. Wherever the mortar between the tiles had hairline cracks from the blistering April sun, he applied hot coal tar, sealing the joints with rhythmic, deliberate strokes.\n\nAs he worked, the first cold gusts of the southwest monsoon would arrive from the river.\n\nThe sky would darken from slate to deep charcoal. Across the vast North Kolkata skyline, thousands of pigeons would wheel in panic toward their roosts, and the smell of ozone, wet dust, and river water would herald the deluge.\n\nThen the rain would hit—a solid wall of grey water that turned the terrace into a foaming lake two inches deep.\n\nNirmal would not hurry downstairs.\n\nHe stood beside the brick parapet, his cotton dhoti soaked and clinging to his knees, his face turned upward into the cool, violent downpour. He watched the water rush toward the six makaras. The iron mouths roared as they spouted six arching jets of stormwater into the alleyway below, functioning with the effortless hydraulic capacity his grandfather had engineered in 1892.\n\n\"The roof holds, Kalyani,\" Nirmal would say aloud into the wind, his voice swallowed by the thunder rolling over Baghbazar. \"The drainage is clear. The brick is dry underneath.\"\n\nHe knew that as long as the roof was sound, the rooms below were immortal.\n\nThe ceilings of Moulmein teak, the ledgers in the library, the ivory dial of the Junghans clock, and the column of height marks on the doorframe would remain safe from the rot that had claimed so many other houses in the neighborhood.\n\nHe would lock the iron gate with the brass key, descend the spiral stairs in the darkness, and step into the dry, scented sanctuary of the first floor, feeling the profound satisfaction of a captain whose vessel has weathered another squall without taking a cup of water into the hold."
