      "chapterTitle": "VI. The Solitude and the Fourth Generation (2017–2022)",
      "heading": "VI. The Solitude and the Fourth Generation (2017–2022)",
      "body": "Grief in an old house does not remain a wound; over the years, it transforms into an architecture of companionship.\n\nAfter Kalyani’s cremation at Nimtala Ghat, Nirmal lived alone at 14 Balaram Ghosh Street for twelve years.\n\nHis children pleaded with him to leave.\n\n\"Baba, you cannot live in that enormous house by yourself,\" Soma begged on the telephone from Bangalore every Sunday evening. \"You are seventy-three years old. If you slip on the marble stairs at midnight, who will find you? Come live with us. We have a ground-floor suite with an attached garden. You can teach math to your granddaughters.\"\n\n\"Move to Ohio, Baba,\" Aniruddha urged from America. \"I will file for your permanent residency tomorrow. The winters are snowy, but the house has radiant floor heating. You can spend your days in my workshop.\"\n\nNirmal listened to both of his children with immense tenderness, but his answer never varied.\n\n\"My dear children,\" he would reply, seated at his father’s teak desk in the library, looking out at the green parijat canopy. \"A banyan tree that has stood eighty years in the soil of North Kolkata cannot be uprooted and planted in a suburban lawn. Its roots are wound around the brick footings of this house. If you take me away from Shyambazar, I will wither into a ghost within six months. Here, every brick remembers your mother. When the south wind blows from the river at night, I hear her footsteps on the verandah. I am not alone. The house is keeping me company.\"\n\nHe kept the mansion with fierce, loving discipline.\n\nEvery Sunday at eight o’clock, the Junghans clock was wound. Every morning at dawn, Nirmal took a stiff broom made of coconut ribs and swept the courtyard flagstones, gathering the fallen parijat blossoms into a brass bowl and placing them before Kalyani’s framed portrait on the library mantelpiece. When the monsoons brought rising damp, he scraped the saltpeter; when the white ants attempted to cross the library threshold, he repelled them with mixtures of neem oil and turpentine.\n\nIn February of 2021, when Nirmal turned seventy-seven, life gave him an unexpected, breathtaking gift.\n\nAniruddha’s daughter, Rimli—now twenty-one, an architecture graduate from the University of Illinois—won a prestigious travel fellowship to document the heritage domestic architecture of colonial Calcutta. She arrived at Balaram Ghosh Street to stay for three months.\n\nAnd with her came her six-month-old daughter, Oindrila.\n\nFor ninety days, the old mansion was blessed with four living generations of human presence.\n\nThe sound of an infant’s babbling echoed through the high lime-plastered arches. Baby clothes fluttered once again from the wrought-iron terrace balustrades. The smell of warm baby powder mingled with the ancient scent of teak and camphor.\n\nOne golden afternoon in late February, Rimli found her grandfather standing in the doorway of the library.\n\nThe winter sun was streaming through the fanlight, casting an amber glow across the left doorframe with its sixty-seven years of family notches.\n\nNirmal was holding baby Oindrila in his arms. The child was laughing, her tiny bare feet kicking against his cotton kurta.\n\n\"Bring me the pocketknife and the purple ink from the desk, Rimli,\" Nirmal said, his voice soft with emotion.\n\nRimli brought the knife and the ink.\n\nNirmal held the baby upright, resting her tiny pink heels against the marble threshold at the bottom of the jamb.\n\n\"Hold her head steady against the timber for just two seconds, little bird,\" Nirmal whispered to his granddaughter.\n\nWith the bone-handled knife, his aged hands remarkably steady, Nirmal carved a tiny, delicate horizontal line into the dark wood, just twenty-four inches above the floor.\n\nWith the dip pen, he wrote beneath it in his precise surveyor’s hand:\n\n*24.02.2021 — Oindrila — 6 mos — 2 ft 0½ in.*\n\nRimli stood beside him, looking at the doorframe.\n\nHer eyes traveled up the column of marks: her grandfather's first notch from 1954; her father's notches from the 1970s; her own mark from 2005; and now her daughter's notch from 2021.\n\nFour generations of one family, carved into four inches of Burma teak, standing victorious against oblivion.\n\nRimli put her arms around her grandfather's frail shoulders and wept against his chest.\n\n\"Dadu,\" she whispered. \"This doorframe is a miracle. It is more sacred than any temple in India.\"\n\nNirmal smiled gently, stroking her hair.\n\n\"No, my child,\" he said softly. \"The wood is just wood. It is the love we bring to it that makes it remember.\"",
