      "id": "sec-hj-11b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "VI-B. The Final Chimes on Balaram Ghosh Street (2023–2024)",
      "heading": "VI-B. The Final Chimes on Balaram Ghosh Street (2023–2024)",
      "body": "The final November arrived in the year 2024.\n\nOn the fourteenth of November, Nirmalendu Roy celebrated his eightieth birthday. He received video calls from Bangalore and Ohio; he saw his great-granddaughter Oindrila running across an autumn lawn in America, shouting \"Dadu!\" into the screen. He smiled, blessed them all, and told them he was well.\n\nIn truth, his body had grown very light and fragile, like a dry bamboo leaf waiting for the first winter wind. His steps on the marble stairs were slow, deliberate, and accompanied by the soft tapping of his ebony walking stick. But his mind remained as clear as the autumn sky above the Hooghly.\n\nOn Sunday, the seventeenth of November, he climbed the footstool at eight in the morning and wound the Junghans clock for the last time.\n\nThe springs took the turn smoothly: *crrk... crrk... crrk... crrk.* The heavy brass pendulum swung with its ancient, unbroken cadence: *tock... tick... tock... tick.*\n\nThree days later, on the morning of the twentieth of November, Nirmal woke at dawn.\n\nHe felt no pain, no fever, no shortness of breath. He felt only a profound, velvety exhaustion—the sensation of a long, heavy ship that has crossed a stormy ocean and finally reached the calm, sheltered waters of its home anchorage.\n\nHe bathed in warm water, put on a fresh white cotton kurta, and walked out into the inner quadrangle.\n\nIt was six o'clock in the morning.\n\nThe parijat tree, seventy-four years after his mother had planted it, was in the glory of its annual bloom. During the night, thousands of flowers had fallen. The grey Chunar flagstones were completely buried beneath an immaculate, snowy blanket of white petals and vivid orange stems. The sweet, heavenly perfume filled the entire courtyard, rising toward the pale morning sky like incense.\n\nNirmal stood beneath the canopy. He bent down, his knees stiff with eighty years of living, and picked up three fallen blossoms.\n\nHe held them in the palm of his right hand.\n\nHe walked into the ground-floor library. He did not sit at his desk. He sat down in his father's old high-backed teak armchair beside the doorframe—the chair in which Dhirendranath had sat in 1944.\n\nThrough the open green louvered shutters, the morning sounds of North Kolkata floated into the quiet room:\n\nThe distant, metallic rumble of the first tram grinding over the rails at Shyambazar five-point crossing.\n\nThe deep, mournful steam whistle of a Port Trust launch heading down the Hooghly toward the sea.\n\nThe cheerful whistling of the boy delivering milk cans on his bicycle.\n\nThe calls of green parakeets swooping down from the parijat tree into the courtyard.\n\nUp on the first floor, the 1928 Junghans clock began to strike eight o'clock.\n\n*Dong... dong... dong... dong... dong... dong... dong... dong.*\n\nThe eight golden chimes resonated through the heavy timber beams, vibrated through the lime-plastered brick walls, and sank deep into the cool flagstones beneath his feet.\n\nNirmal closed his eyes.\n\nHe did not feel fear. He did not feel regret. He felt only an immense, infinite gratitude for the eighty years he had been allowed to inhabit this earth, this city, this river, and this house.\n\nHe saw the blackout curtains falling in 1947; he saw the southern roof in 1956; he saw Kalyani’s red footprints in 1968; he saw the floating white blossoms in the flood of 1978; he saw four generations of children standing against the teak doorframe.\n\nHis breathing slowed, softened, and gently ceased.\n\nHis right hand remained open on the wooden armrest of the chair, cradling the three parijat flowers in the golden morning light.\n\nWhen the neighbor's son came at nine with the morning newspaper, he found the old surveyor sitting peacefully in his chair, with a faint, serene smile upon his lips.\n\nAround him, 14 Balaram Ghosh Street stood silent, sheltering, and faithful.\n\nThe brick held his warmth; the teak held his measurements; the clock kept his time; and the parijat showered its blossoms upon the stones that had known his name for eighty years."
